================================================================================
FORM STATE STRUCTURE
================================================================================

formData:

{
  name: '',
  email: ''
}


Meaning:

formData.name
    ↓
Name Input


formData.email
    ↓
Email Input


================================================================================
WHY USE AN OBJECT?
================================================================================

These values belong to the same form.

Using:

{
  name: '',
  email: ''
}

provides:

• One form-data structure

• Clear field names

• Easy expansion

• One generic change handler

• Simple debugging


However, an object is not mandatory.

For very small independent fields, separate useState calls can also be valid.

The object approach is especially convenient when many fields use the same
change pattern.


================================================================================
STATE SETUP TABLE
================================================================================

+----------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------+
| ITEM                 | TYPE                  | PURPOSE                      |
+----------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------+
| formData             | Object                | Current form values          |
| formData.name        | String                | Name value                   |
| formData.email       | String                | Email value                  |
| setFormData          | Function              | Requests state update        |
+----------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------+


================================================================================
PART 4: CREATING THE onChange HANDLER
================================================================================

WHAT DOES onChange DO?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

For a controlled text input:

1. User enters text

2. React receives the change event

3. Handler reads the input value

4. Handler requests a state update

5. React renders using the current state

6. Input value reflects state


================================================================================
CREATE THE HANDLER
================================================================================

import { useState } from 'react'
import './SimpleForm.css'


export default function SimpleForm() {

  const [formData, setFormData] =
    useState({

      name: '',

      email: ''

    })


  const handleChange = (event) => {

    const {
      name,
      value
    } = event.target


    console.log(
      `Field: ${name}, Value: ${value}`
    )


    setFormData(previousData => ({

      ...previousData,

      [name]: value

    }))
  }


  return (
    <div className="simple-form-container">

      <h1>
        Simple Form
      </h1>

    </div>
  )
}


================================================================================
HOW handleChange WORKS
================================================================================

Suppose the user types:

John

into:

name="name"


EVENT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

event.target.name

=
"name"


event.target.value

=
"John"


STATE UPDATE:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setFormData(previousData => ({

  ...previousData,

  [name]: value

}))


Becomes conceptually:

{
  ...previousData,

  name: 'John'
}


If previous state was:

{
  name: '',
  email: ''
}


The resulting state becomes:

{
  name: 'John',
  email: ''
}


================================================================================
HANDLER EXPLANATION
================================================================================

+--------------------------+---------------------------------------------+
| CODE                     | PURPOSE                                     |
+--------------------------+---------------------------------------------+
| handleChange(event)      | Handles input changes                       |
| event.target             | Input that generated event                  |
| name                     | Identifies corresponding state property     |
| value                    | Current input value                         |
| ...previousData          | Copies existing form values                 |
| [name]                   | Computed property                           |
| [name]: value            | Updates matching field                      |
| setFormData(...)         | Requests next form state                    |
+--------------------------+---------------------------------------------+


================================================================================
WHY USE THE SPREAD OPERATOR?
================================================================================

Suppose:

{
  name: 'John',
  email: 'john@example.com'
}


Without spreading existing values:

setFormData({
  name: 'Jane'
})


Result:

{
  name: 'Jane'
}


email is missing.


WITH SPREAD:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setFormData(previousData => ({

  ...previousData,

  name: 'Jane'

}))


Result:

{
  name: 'Jane',
  email: 'john@example.com'
}


================================================================================
WHY FUNCTIONAL STATE UPDATE?
================================================================================

This is valid in many simple handlers:

setFormData({
  ...formData,
  [name]: value
})


But because the next object is based on the previous object, this form is
robust and explicit:

setFormData(previousData => ({

  ...previousData,

  [name]: value

}))


MEMORY:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

NEXT STATE DEPENDS ON PREVIOUS STATE
        ↓
FUNCTIONAL STATE SETTER


